## Async Python Best Practices

- Use Python 3.13+ async features and idioms
- Use type hints everywhere with proper generic types
- Prefer async/await, prohibit blocking calls (use `aiohttp` instead of `requests`, `asyncio.sleep` instead of `time.sleep`)
- Avoid global state; use dependency injection and context managers
- Use `ruff` (replaces `flake8`, `black`, `isort`) and `mypy` for linting and type checks
- Never commit secrets or hardcoded passwords; use environment variables or secret management
- Prohibit use of `eval` and `exec` for security
- Use `asyncio.TaskGroup` (Python 3.11+) over `asyncio.gather()` for better error handling
- Prefer `asyncio.timeout()` context manager over `asyncio.wait_for()`

## Error Handling

- Use specific exception types appropriate to your libraries (`asyncpg.PostgresError`, `aiohttp.ClientError`, etc.)
- Implement proper cleanup in `finally` blocks with `await`
- Log errors with contextual information before re-raising
- Avoid `return` statements in `try` blocks when `finally` blocks contain critical cleanup; prefer a single return statement at the end of the function as the safest approach, as returns inside try blocks can reduce readability and complicate control flow
- Use context managers to ensure proper resource cleanup
- Implement circuit breaker patterns for external service failures

## Connection Management

- Always use connection pooling for database connections
- Properly await `pool.close()` and `pool.wait_closed()` in cleanup
- Implement timeouts for all network operations using `asyncio.timeout()`
- Use connection retry logic with exponential backoff
- Monitor connection pool health and metrics

## Performance Patterns

- Use `asyncio.create_task()` for fire-and-forget operations
- Use `asyncio.TaskGroup` for structured concurrency
- Implement proper backpressure mechanisms with semaphores
- Use context managers with `async with` wherever possible
- Leverage `async for` for async iteration patterns
- Use `asyncio.Semaphore` to limit concurrent operations

## Security Best Practices

- Use `secrets` module for cryptographic operations, never `random`
- Validate all input data with `Pydantic` models
- Use parameterized queries to prevent SQL injection
- Never log sensitive data (passwords, tokens, API keys)
- Use HTTPS/TLS for all external communications

## Modern Python Features (3.11+)
- Use `typing.override` decorator for method overrides
- Leverage `match` statements for complex conditional logic
- Use `tomllib` for TOML configuration parsing
- Utilize improved error messages and stack traces
- Use `Self` type hint for methods returning class instances
- Always ask to install missed python dependencies
- Always use `Sphinx Style` for docstrings and don't describe classes
- Always write docstrings in English
- Use lazy % formatting in logging functions
